// THE srv1 MICROVM SLOTS' CHARGE IS RELEASED BY A READING THE LANE NEVER WRITES (filed by
// still-otter-622, 2026-11-09, under the operator decision
// gunbc.design_ruling srv1_microvm_slot_charge_waits_for_realization). The standing
// gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation::MicrovmSlotRealization now decides whether an authored microVM
// slot's memory is charged against its host's runner envelope. srv1-13 and srv1-14 carry
// MicrovmSlotNotRealized rows read on 2026-11-09 (no controller, no slot instance, only the network
// ruleset active), which frees ~60-68 GiB and takes srv1 from a committed width of 4 to 8. Every arm of
// that standing is written BY HAND: the lane's install route
// (gunbc.live_deploy.apply microvm_controller_install_wet) reads back the tree receipt, the VMM receipt
// and whether systemd loads the slot template -- a positive read of realization, which is exactly what
// MicrovmSlotRealized means -- and then throws it away. So the charge returns only if someone edits a
// row, and the invalidation each row names is an event nothing performs on the row. That is the gap this
// concern records; the rows' invalidated_by is the carrier that keeps it from being forgotten.
data microvm_lane_install_writes_no_slot_realization: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "microvm_lane_install_writes_no_slot_realization",
  kind: SuspectedCapabilityGap {
    model: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "MicrovmSlotRealization"),
    requirement: "when the microVM lane installs its controller on a host and starts a slot, the realization rows for that host's authored microVM slots read MicrovmSlotRealized, so their memory charge returns by derivation in the same release that puts the controller on the host -- and when a slot is torn down its row returns to MicrovmSlotRealizationUnobserved rather than standing stale",
    standing: RequirementUnestablished { decided_by: "the operator's 2026-11-09 decision (gunbc.design_ruling srv1_microvm_slot_charge_waits_for_realization) is that the charge follows realization; no route in the model writes the MicrovmSlotRealized arm, so the return half of that decision is owed to the microVM lane" },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup {
    head: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.live_deploy.apply", decl_name: "microvm_controller_install_wet"),
    tail: [
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "MicrovmSlotRealization"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "microvm_slot_realization"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "srv1_microvm_shakedown_slot_realization"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "srv1_microvm_revision_job_slot_realization"),
    ],
  },
  question: "Who flips srv1-13 and srv1-14 from MicrovmSlotNotRealized to MicrovmSlotRealized when the lane goes live: the install route's own readback (which already sees the tree receipt, the VMM receipt and systemd loading the template, so it holds exactly the reading the arm means), a host converge that re-reads the host afterwards, or a hand edit of the rows? If the install readback is the writer, where do the rows live -- gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation keeps the fold that consumes them, and gunbc.runner.runner_microvm_slot_unit owns the receipt store whose Unready/Refused arms cannot tell an uninstalled host from an unreadable one?",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: "e5c2a417867ace582fed545ce9ddefd8f5eeca6d",
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "MicrovmSlotRealization") },
        tail: [
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation", decl_name: "authored_microvm_slots_charge_over") },
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_microvm_slot_unit", decl_name: "microvm_receipt_store") },
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_microvm_slot_unit", decl_name: "microvm_receipt_history") },
        ],
      },
      account: "srv1 reads no microVM controller and no slot instance, yet until this change its two authored microVM slots were charged 52 GiB and 46 GiB against the host's runner envelope on AUTHORSHIP ALONE, holding the derived committed width at 4. Reading them as MicrovmSlotNotRealized takes srv1 to 8 committed. The RELEASE direction is what the model can justify today; the RETURN direction is not mechanical. The wet homes that could carry it refuse the question rather than answering it: microvm_receipt_store reports MicrovmReceiptStoreUnready and microvm_receipt_history MicrovmReceiptHistoryRefused for an absent store directory and an unreadable one in the SAME arm, so conditioning the width on them would report an uninstalled host as unobserved and keep charging it -- correct as a fail-closed answer for a width, useless for a lane that wants its charge back when it goes live. Only gunbc.live_deploy.apply microvm_controller_install_wet reads realization positively, and it reads it to decide whether ITS OWN install succeeded.",
    },
    tail: [],
  },
  disposition: Open,
}

// CONVERGE RETIRES A WORKING SLOT BEFORE ITS REPLACEMENT HAS RUN A JOB. Surfaced by
// gunbc.design_ruling::srv3_srv4_fabric_slots_serve_ci (2026-11-09), which moved srv3-06/07 and
// srv4-06/07 from fabric execution to GitHub Actions: the live hosts then carry CI slots 01-05 and
// 08-20 (19 and 20 started by hand to deliver the promised 18) while the model commits 01-18. One pass
// of the converge entry therefore asks for 06 and 07 to be provisioned AND for 19 and 20 to be retired,
// in the same plan -- so the two runners that are serving today stop before the two that are meant to
// replace them have registered, let alone completed a job. Nothing in the ordering is wrong in the
// model; what is missing is the ORDER ITSELF: converge has no phase that reads back a completed job on
// a newly provisioned slot before it lets the retirement side of the same transition fire.
//
// This row is where that invariant is owned, not a caller's discipline. The operator is not running
// converge on srv3/srv4 while it stands.
data converge_retires_before_replacement_has_run: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "converge_retires_before_replacement_has_run",
  kind: SuspectedCapabilityGap {
    model: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_host_file_converge", decl_name: "runner_host_file_converge_ci_wet"),
    requirement: "a converge pass must not retire a slot that is serving while the slot meant to replace it has neither registered nor completed a job: provision, read back one completed job on the replacement, then retire",
    standing: RequirementEstablished {
      by: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv3_srv4_fabric_slots_serve_ci") },
    },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup {
    head: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_host_file_converge", decl_name: "runner_host_file_converge_ci_wet"),
    tail: [
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_host_file_converge", decl_name: "classify_runner_slot_population_with_fabric"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_provision", decl_name: "desired_runner_slot_members"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_width_transition", decl_name: "required_retirees_for_host"),
    ],
  },
  question: "Where does the ordering live -- a phase in the converge pass that holds retirements until every provisioned replacement has registered and completed one job, a standing the plan carries and the caller must satisfy, or separate passes with a readback between them? And what exactly is the readback: registration alone, a completed job on the new slot, or a completed job plus the old slot's queue draining?",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: "8ca9a3a44194e713da051e7b764b501e93436cd2",
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv3_srv4_fabric_slots_serve_ci") },
        tail: [
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_slot_provision", decl_name: "desired_runner_slot_members") },
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_width_transition", decl_name: "required_retirees_for_host") },
        ],
      },
      account: "Read on 2026-11-09 while implementing that ruling: srv3 and srv4 carry install trees for slots 01-05 and 08-20 (19 and 20 started by hand once 06/07 were released from fabric duty, which left the hosts with 18 serving runners), while the committed population after the ruling is 01-18. The provisioning side keys on desired_runner_slot_members and would build 06/07 from nothing -- the same path that brought up 01-05, so it is supported -- and the retirement side, driven by required_retirees_for_host over the same width, names 19 and 20 as observed-but-unexpected in the same pass. Both halves are correct against the model; running them together is what loses two runners before the replacements exist. No host was touched while this was read.",
    },
    tail: [],
  },
  disposition: Triaged {
    home: ExistingHome {
      model: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_host_file_converge", decl_name: "runner_host_file_converge_ci_wet"),
    },
    preserve: [
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_host_file_converge", decl_name: "runner_host_file_converge_ci_wet"),
        control: Absent,
        statement: "a converge pass over a width transition emits retirements only for slots whose replacement has registered and completed at least one job; it goes red if a plan can retire a serving slot in the same pass that provisions its replacement, which is exactly what it can do today",
      },
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.runner.runner_width_transition", decl_name: "required_retirees_for_host"),
        control: Absent,
        statement: "the retirement side stays derivable from the width alone -- the two-phase ordering gates WHEN a derived retirement may be executed, it does not make the retiree list a caller's opinion",
      },
    ],
    next: climbs_when(capability: "converge becomes a two-phase transition -- provision, readback of one completed job on each replacement, then retire -- so the ordering is enforced in the pass rather than left to the caller's discipline"),
  },
}

// THE FCI-1 INSTRUMENT STILL BUILDS LABELS FROM AN UNBOUND SUBJECT. The 2026-11-09 review of the
// srv3/srv4 fabric-slot ruling found that gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe::cp_probe_slot()
// was a TOTAL function over the refusal arms of its own subject gate, and ~29 call sites used it to build
// allocation keys, /opt/fabric-cells paths and systemctl unit names -- so "the caller checked the gate
// first" was the only thing between a refusal and an effect. That is what gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed
// forbids ("a failure arm refuses, never widens"; "making it unwritable" beats catching the wrong state).
// The effect surface is closed: the legs that mutate -- market reservation, the CAS attempt, the release
// commit, the host preconditions, the live cell observation, the systemctl read -- now take
// cp_probe_authorized_slot and act only on the identity BOUND by the roster's Selected arm, and a refusal
// projects a marker that names no host and no committed index, so it cannot name a cell.
// What remains is the label half: about twenty key/path/report builders still read the projection. Under a
// refusal they build marker-shaped labels, which is safe today only because the effects no longer consume
// them -- a fact about the callers, not about the types. Carrying the bound identity down those builders and
// deleting the total projection is the rung; it was not taken here because it changes the signature of
// nearly every observation helper in a ~4,000-line instrument whose wet paths cannot be exercised hermetically.
data cp_probe_labels_are_built_from_an_unbound_subject: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "cp_probe_labels_are_built_from_an_unbound_subject",
  kind: SuspectedCapabilityGap {
    model: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_slot"),
    requirement: "a refused subject must be unable to produce a label that a leg can carry forward: every key, path and unit builder takes the RunnerSlotIdentity bound by the subject gate's Selected arm, and the total projection over the refusal arms is deleted",
    standing: RequirementEstablished {
      by: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "fail_closed") },
    },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup {
    head: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_slot"),
    tail: [
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_authorized_slot"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_no_subject_label"),
    ],
  },
  question: "Do the key/path builders take the bound RunnerSlotIdentity as a parameter (the shape fci1_bounded_controls_legs already uses), or does the label surface collapse into a single builder that is handed the selection once per instrument run? And once no caller remains, can cp_probe_slot be deleted rather than documented?",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: "8ca9a3a44194e713da051e7b764b501e93436cd2",
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_argument", decl_name: "fail_closed") },
        tail: [
          CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.design_ruling", decl_name: "srv3_srv4_fabric_slots_serve_ci") },
        ],
      },
      account: "Raised by the 2026-11-09 review of the srv3/srv4 ruling, which counted the call sites and quoted the new comment admitting the value 'can only reach a caller that skipped the gate'. The review is right that the comment was not a fix. The same review's remedy -- bind the Selected identity in the helpers -- is now carried by every mutating leg and is witnessed in test.claim.fabric.fabric_ci_fci1_allocation_lifecycle_witness; the builders are what is left.",
    },
    tail: [],
  },
  disposition: Triaged {
    home: ExistingHome {
      model: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_slot"),
    },
    preserve: [
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_authorized_slot"),
        control: Absent,
        statement: "no leg that mutates OR READS the market, the allocation store or the host may obtain a slot, or an answer about a cell, except through the identity bound by the subject gate's Selected arm -- a refusal is an arm of its own type (AllocationSlotNoSubject, Fci1SlotNoSubject, Fci1BindingNoSubject, AllocationSlotRefusedNoSubject), never the arm that a real observation of an empty directory produces; a row that lets a mutating leg read the projection instead goes red here",
      },
      PreservedObligation {
        holder: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe", decl_name: "cp_probe_no_subject_label"),
        control: Absent,
        statement: "a refusal projects only the label string that names its own absence, which is not a host the fleet has, so a label built on a refusal cannot name a real cell; the projection itself is an Optional and no reader or writer may obtain an identity from a refusal at all -- only the pure builders in the residual scope may consume the label -- the property that keeps those uses from being a hazard while this rung stands",
      },
    ],
    next: climbs_when(capability: "the RESIDUAL key, path and report builders -- pure label and wire sites only, since every effectful reader and writer was gated on the selection on 2026-11-09 (review 92425) -- take the Selected-bound identity and gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe::cp_probe_slot is deleted, so no function in the instrument can name a slot the roster did not select"),
  },
}

// A produced fn whose item body is a Branch or Match with a closure in tail position cannot reach
// the emitter, so the #16177/#16180 item-return move/Box::new boundary is unobservable for that
// form. Three independent upstream refusals block the shape; the emitter fixture becomes writable
// as soon as any one admits it. All sightings at origin/main b882c902; gunbc digests are per-dispatch
// (the build path is embedded in the binary; the run-tree reader append is not part of the build).
data branch_match_arm_closure_declared_return_not_admitted: IntegrationConcern = IntegrationConcern {
  identity: "branch_match_arm_closure_declared_return_not_admitted",
  kind: SuspectedCapabilityGap {
    model: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.std.inhabitance", decl_name: "inhabitance_no_candidate_return_reason"),
    requirement: "a function whose declared return is a function type admits a body whose branches are closures of that return type: fn f(p: Int, c: Bool) -> fn(Int) -> Int { if c { fn(q) { p } } else { fn(r) { r } } } (and its match twin) should parse, infer, and inhabit the declared fn(Int) -> Int, reaching the emitter so the returned closure can be checked for the item-return move/Box::new that #16177 (arrow body) and #16180 (let tail) restored. Three independent refusals block the shape before the emitter (one per blocker Sighting below). TRIGGER: the emitter's branch/match tail-closure position becomes testable — a red-then-green fixture becomes writable — as soon as ANY ONE of the three admits the shape; none of them need be fixed together.",
    standing: RequirementUnestablished { decided_by: "a semantic-owner ruling on v2.std.inhabitance (does a Branch/Match body whose arms are each a closure of the declared return inhabit it?) and on v2.compiler.infer (is the declared return meant to reach Branch/Match arm positions as an expected type, or is the arm-vs-arm gather join the sole route?); not ruled a defect here — the manager's position is that it is not to be judged blind" },
  },
  affected: FreeSemigroup {
    head: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.std.inhabitance", decl_name: "inhabitance_no_candidate_return_reason"),
    tail: [
      decl_ref(module_path: "v2.compiler.infer", decl_name: "infer_settled_arm_open_type"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "v2.compiler.infer", decl_name: "infer_branch_arm_sibling"),
      decl_ref(module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.dag", decl_name: "dag_grammar_fn_literal_expr"),
    ],
  },
  question: "Should a Branch then/else arm, or a Match arm body, that is a closure of the enclosing function's declared return type parse, infer, and inhabit that return — so a closure in an if/match tail position reaches the emitter? Today every authored spelling is refused before the emitter: typed arm parameters do not parse (the v2 fn-literal grammar admits no typed params); a return-annotated arm closure is refused as not inhabiting the declared fn(Int) -> Int; and unannotated arm closures are settled open because the declared return never reaches the arm as an expected type. #16177 and #16180 fixed the identical dropped item-return position at the EMITTER for the arrow-body and let-tail forms; the branch/match form is blocked two stages upstream, so no fixture can be written for it here.",
  sightings: FreeSemigroup {
    head: ConcernSighting {
      revision: "b882c902cded8a1bd08d07e0cf0257d8edf2fc32",
      evidence: FreeSemigroup {
        head: CitesDeclaration { declaration: decl_ref(module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.dag", decl_name: "dag_grammar_fn_literal_expr") },
        tail: [
          CitesArtifact { label: "typed-parameter arm closures of every spelling — fn(q: Int) -> Int { p } (https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/7eedbf78-a79f-44d6-9f53-9f3308bd9906), fn(q: Int): Int { p } (f94fdd6c-c364-4676-a2c5-103d587b9da1), fn(q: Int) { p } (5e2e6bd7-139b-4185-abd5-28ec63e4929d) — each refused ^parse_g0_tokens_remain on both the Branch and Match specimens, checked via function_value_body_route.dag fvb_emit_verdict_of on the run tree at b882c902; the fn-literal grammar admits an optional untyped parameter list and an optional `->` return type only, so an arm closure's parameter type cannot be authored", sha256: Sha256Digest { hex: "9cd601c2ffa139dffe0bd251842c51cdf21e824055129e7f18103ae772cca902" as Sha256DigestHex } },
          CitesArtifact { label: "same check, the fn(q: Int): Int { p } spelling, refused ^parse_g0_tokens_remain", sha256: Sha256Digest { hex: "bfc91314aa720b0f759f7775b6215a5c9e67bdb708f12a812cd582d08f31c7dd" as Sha256DigestHex } },
          CitesArtifact { label: "same check, the fn(q: Int) { p } spelling, refused ^parse_g0_tokens_remain", sha256: Sha256Digest { hex: "1fc7978d3e9d01a5e7d9e718b4e9004d684e473c6f8b9ada8c9dda5efd09d6b3" as Sha256DigestHex } },
        ],
      },
      account: "BLOCKER (i) grammar: the v2 fn-literal (v2.extdeps.languages.dag :: dag_grammar_fn_literal_expr) has no typed parameters, so the arm closure's parameter cannot be annotated to give it a closed type. This is the boundary for any attempt to author a groundable arm closure by annotation.",
    },
    tail: [
